{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793553349,"nonce":"9/mjwEExTwsy+7pRwr60hciycis=","message":"c0953368: an entry-counted promotion waits for a free core instead of 20 ms of baseline quiet. NUC, 4 pinned P cores (taskset -c 0,2,4,6, so available_parallelism is 4, as on CI), --fast, interleaved, vs ZYNTAX_OPTIMIZE_QUIET_MS=20: raytrace 10.8 -\u003e 9.8 ms (-9%), scimark_sor -7%, go -8%; deltablue +3.6%, spectral +3.8%, nbody_modified +1.5% (22 kernels, 5 runs, score geomean 0.994, cpu +3.6%). zylua 17 benchmarks 0.984. Mac, unpinned: raytrace -20%, deltablue -7%, nbody_modified +31% (ee27afa). A 0 ms window gives the same on raytrace and SOR but deltablue +13% (its first iteration's baseline compiles then fight the installs for the cores); the 5 ms window is the same as 20.\n\nWhat the data says about the rest of the issue:\n- A separate gate thread holding entry-counted requests while loop requests go straight to the broker made raytrace 40% slower: the render loop's regions (35 + 31 ms of LLVM) overtook the hot leaf methods, and the broker is busy with them for the whole window. Order matters in the other direction here: leaves first. In SOR it is the reverse: the loop's region inlines its callees, so five 2.5 ms callee installs ahead of it cost 12 ms of a 270 ms run.\n- Lowering the broker thread to nice 10 or 19 made no difference (arms differed by the portable-code effect, see 51eebdd).\n- MCJIT code-generation level (Aggressive/Default/Less) changes neither install time nor speed; LLVM's own optimisation is 65% of an install (30.8 of 47.6 ms for SOR's loop).\n- After the rule, raytrace's remaining gap is install throughput: ~28 installs, 128 ms in sequence on the one broker, the large ones (Scene$_lightIsVisible 24 ms, rayColour 17, zb_any_arith 18 at 0.5% of the time) landing in iterations 6 to 9. The LLVM backend is one Mutex\u003cLLVMJitBackend\u003e, so two installs cannot overlap without a second backend and context.\n- Marking calls to cold functions cold (Cranelift set_cold_block for blocks calling a cold callee; LLVM `cold` on the call site, which the cross-tier indirect calls lack today): no gain. -1% and +0.5% separately, +1% together over 7 runs.","files":null}]}